test_pmtuicmp — RFC 1191 ICMP-driven PMTU cache update
=========================================================

Second half of Option B's discovery half. Drives a real
ICMP type 3 code 4 ("Fragmentation Needed") frame through
`inet_handle_icmp_dest_unreach` via the test injector, then
reads the post-update PMTU cache to assert the parser pulled
the Next-Hop MTU out of the ICMP body and called
`inet_pmtu_set` for the inner-IP destination.

This pairs with `test_frag` (Session 1): the cache is now
populatable from the wire, not just from the test-only
`pmtu_inject_test` setter.

Pass criterion: prints `pmtuicmp: ok`.

    module oskit.bin.test_pmtuicmp

    import oskit.ulib.{puts, exit, exit_with}
    import oskit.net.{pmtu_discover_inject}


    print_idec(v: int)
        var n = v
        if n < 0
            puts("-")
            n = -n
        if n >= 10
            print_idec(n / 10)
        var b: [1]byte
        b[0] = byte('0' + (n % 10))
        puts(string(&b[0], 1))


    fail(code: int, got: int)
        puts("pmtuicmp: bad ")
        print_idec(code)
        puts(" got=")
        print_idec(got)
        puts("\n")
        exit_with(code)


    main(argv: []string)

Inject a synthesized ICMP type-3 code-4 telling us the path
to 10.0.2.99 has an MTU of 600. The cache should latch that
exact value (above INET_PMTU_MIN=296, below INET_LINK_MTU=1500
so no clamp).

        val cached1 = pmtu_discover_inject(10, 0, 2, 99, 600)
        if cached1 < 0
            fail(1, cached1)
            return
        if cached1 != 600
            fail(2, cached1)
            return

A second smaller value should overwrite the entry (RFC 1191
§3 — PMTUD lowers monotonically per black-hole-avoidance, but
slix's cache is a plain key-value map; that's fine here since
in real life a router only sends a smaller value than a prior
fragment-needed reply, never a larger one. The test asserts
overwrite either direction.)

        val cached2 = pmtu_discover_inject(10, 0, 2, 99, 400)
        if cached2 < 0
            fail(3, cached2)
            return
        if cached2 != 400
            fail(4, cached2)
            return

A different destination gets its own slot. This catches a
bug where the lookup ignores dst_ip and clobbers a single
shared entry.

        val cached3 = pmtu_discover_inject(192, 168, 99, 1, 800)
        if cached3 < 0
            fail(5, cached3)
            return
        if cached3 != 800
            fail(6, cached3)
            return

The first destination's entry must still hold its 400-byte
value — proves the cache is keyed by IP, not first-write-wins.

        val cached4 = pmtu_discover_inject(10, 0, 2, 99, 0)
        if cached4 < 0
            fail(7, cached4)
            return
        // mtu=0 in the inject means "wire format with zero MTU"
        // — the parser bails before calling inet_pmtu_set per the
        // RFC 792 fallback path, so the prior 400-byte entry
        // survives.
        if cached4 != 400
            fail(8, cached4)
            return

A value below INET_PMTU_MIN clamps to the floor.

        val cached5 = pmtu_discover_inject(10, 0, 2, 99, 100)
        if cached5 < 0
            fail(9, cached5)
            return
        if cached5 != 296
            fail(10, cached5)
            return

A value above INET_LINK_MTU clamps down.

        val cached6 = pmtu_discover_inject(10, 0, 2, 99, 9000)
        if cached6 < 0
            fail(11, cached6)
            return
        if cached6 != 1500
            fail(12, cached6)
            return

        puts("pmtuicmp: ok\n")
        exit()
